  ],
  "answer": "<p>The correct choice is <b>db-n2-highmem-32 with 4 TB of SSD storage</b> because it meets the projected workload while staying cost efficient.</p><p>Current peaks of 4200 queries per second and 2.5 TB of data are expected to grow to roughly 6700 queries per second and about 3.1 TB of data over the next year. A 4 TB SSD allocation provides enough capacity for the data growth plus room for indexes, temporary space, and maintenance operations. SSD is recommended for high throughput transactional workloads because it delivers the IOPS and latency needed for sustained query rates and predictable performance. Storage auto increase can be enabled to provide headroom without overcommitting on day one.</p><p>The n2 high-memory profile at 32 vCPUs gives a strong balance of compute and RAM so caches and buffers can serve more reads and the instance can tolerate large numbers of connections. If connection surges occur you can use connection pooling to manage client concurrency while keeping instance resources efficient. This configuration therefore aligns with the performance target and cost control goals.</p><p><i>db-n2-standard-32 with 6 TB of HDD storage</i> is not appropriate for high query rates because HDD throughput and IOPS are significantly lower than SSD which leads to latency under load. It also allocates less memory per vCPU than a high-memory profile which reduces cache effectiveness.</p><p><i>db-n2-highmem-64 with 6 TB of SSD storage</i> would handle the workload but it is overprovisioned for the forecast and increases cost unnecessarily. You can meet the targets with the smaller high-memory node and still enable storage auto increase for growth.</p><p><i>db-n2-highmem-16 with 3 TB of SSD storage</i> is likely to be constrained on CPU and memory at the forecasted query rate and the storage size leaves too little buffer over the expected 3.1 TB which risks running short once overhead is considered.</p>",
